1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,041
Lesson 4.2.

2
00:00:02,421 --> 00:00:03,703
The Ising model.

3
00:00:04,817 --> 00:00:09,154
If we had to choose a single model for
this course, it would be the Ising model.

4
00:00:09,534 --> 00:00:13,481
Spins that are plus one or
minus one, coupled in pairs.

5
00:00:13,861 --> 00:00:17,052
It is the native program of
a pbit chip, the heart of

6
00:00:17,052 --> 00:00:20,002
Hopfield networks and
Boltzmann machines, and the

7
00:00:20,002 --> 00:00:23,435
language in which many
optimization problems are written.

8
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:27,298
Think of a magnet as a
lattice of tiny compasses.

9
00:00:27,678 --> 00:00:29,828
Each one prefers to
point the same way as its

10
00:00:29,828 --> 00:00:31,834
neighbors, because that lowers the energy.

11
00:00:32,214 --> 00:00:34,327
Temperature shakes them at random.

12
00:00:34,707 --> 00:00:37,775
At low temperature order
wins and large aligned

13
00:00:37,775 --> 00:00:41,039
domains appear; at high
temperature disorder wins.

14
00:00:41,419 --> 00:00:45,763
Between the two regimes there is an
abrupt change: a phase transition.

15
00:00:46,883 --> 00:00:54,771
The energy is E = −Σ
J_ij s_i s_j − Σ h_i s_i.

16
00:00:55,151 --> 00:00:59,770
A positive coupling rewards
aligned spins: it is ferromagnetic.

17
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:03,979
A negative one rewards opposite
spins: it is antiferromagnetic.

18
00:01:04,359 --> 00:01:07,462
The field h_i pushes
each spin toward a value.

19
00:01:07,842 --> 00:01:13,729
What each spin feels is its local
field, u_i = Σ_j J_ij s_j + h_i, and we

20
00:01:13,729 --> 00:01:18,889
will see that a pbit is exactly
a spin responding to that field.

21
00:01:20,017 --> 00:01:22,618
Let's simulate a thirty-two by thirty-two

22
00:01:22,618 --> 00:01:25,537
square lattice with
coupling one and no field.

23
00:01:25,917 --> 00:01:29,836
At temperature 1.5 almost
all spins point the same way.

24
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:34,312
Near 2.27 domains of all sizes appear.

25
00:01:34,691 --> 00:01:36,826
At 3.5 the pattern is noise.

26
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:43,150
The mean magnetization falls from
one to zero around the critical

27
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:49,004
temperature, which Onsager computed
exactly: T_c = 2/ln(1 + √2).

28
00:01:50,117 --> 00:01:54,747
Now let's put three spins on a triangle
with antiferromagnetic couplings.

29
00:01:55,127 --> 00:01:58,294
Each pair wants to be opposite,
but with three spins it

30
00:01:58,294 --> 00:02:01,232
is impossible: at least
one pair stays unsatisfied.

31
00:02:01,612 --> 00:02:04,205
That is called frustration,
and it produces

32
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:06,618
many ground states with the same energy.

33
00:02:06,998 --> 00:02:11,142
If the couplings are random
across a whole lattice, we have a

34
00:02:11,142 --> 00:02:15,150
spin glass: a rugged landscape
with very many local minima.

35
00:02:15,530 --> 00:02:19,615
Finding its global minimum is,
in general, a hard problem.

36
00:02:20,717 --> 00:02:22,644
Let's compare simulation and theory.

37
00:02:23,024 --> 00:02:26,679
The exact critical temperature is 2.269.

38
00:02:27,059 --> 00:02:31,223
At temperature two,
Onsager's formula gives a

39
00:02:31,223 --> 00:02:36,869
magnetization of 0.911, and the
Gibbs simulation gives 0.913.

40
00:02:37,249 --> 00:02:42,232
At 1.5, 0.986 versus 0.987.

41
00:02:42,612 --> 00:02:45,791
Near the critical temperature
the finite lattice rounds

42
00:02:45,791 --> 00:02:48,509
off the transition: it
is a finite-size effect.

43
00:02:49,617 --> 00:02:54,967
The Ising model is the program of pbit
chips, the language of many optimization

44
00:02:54,967 --> 00:02:59,572
problems, and the basis of Hopfield
memories and Boltzmann machines.

45
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:03,371
And near the critical
temperature, as we will

46
00:03:03,371 --> 00:03:06,487
see in lesson 4.4, sampling becomes slow.

47
00:03:07,617 --> 00:03:11,134
Common mistakes: getting
the coupling sign wrong.

48
00:03:11,514 --> 00:03:13,092
Counting each pair twice.

49
00:03:13,472 --> 00:03:16,443
Expecting an abrupt
transition in a finite system.

50
00:03:16,823 --> 00:03:19,228
And forgetting that the
one-dimensional chain

51
00:03:19,228 --> 00:03:21,473
has no transition at positive temperature.

52
00:03:22,583 --> 00:03:28,351
In summary: the Ising model has binary
spins, pairwise couplings and fields.

53
00:03:28,731 --> 00:03:35,409
In two dimensions it has an
order–disorder transition at T_c ≈ 2.27.

54
00:03:35,789 --> 00:03:39,127
And with mixed-sign couplings
frustration appears,

55
00:03:39,127 --> 00:03:42,130
creating rugged landscapes
and hard problems.

56
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:45,751
To practice: compute a local field.

57
00:03:46,131 --> 00:03:49,320
Find the ground states of
the frustrated triangle.

58
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:51,599
Evaluate Onsager's formula.

59
00:03:51,979 --> 00:03:55,289
And explain why the one-dimensional
chain does not order.
